          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What the Most Efficient Label Adds
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Label Requires</h3>
          <p>
            ENERGY STAR Most Efficient is an annual recognition for the top performers within certified products. For 2025, EPA proposed minimums of 1.85 L/kWh for portables up to 25 pints, 2.20 for 25 to 50 pints, 3.40 above 50 pints, and 2.35 or 3.81 for whole-home units depending on cabinet size. The Most Efficient models in the November 2026 dataset line up with those thresholds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who Earns It</h3>
          <p>
            Look at who earns it and a pattern appears. Of the 135 Most Efficient models, many are small online brands sharing identical factory model numbers; one 25-pint design appears under more than a dozen different brand names. Among names most buyers will recognize, the list includes the Levoit LDH-H251S, TCL's T25DC9R, Whirlpool's WHAD251AW, Waykar's CTH150 series, AEOCKY's LEO and RHEA lines, AlorAir's Helios D35 and Sentinel Pro35X, Aprilaire's E080, E100 and E130, and several Santa Fe whole-house models. None of the mainstream Frigidaire, GE, Midea, Hisense or LG portables carried it in that snapshot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is It Worth Paying For?</h3>
          <p>
            Is it worth paying for? Moving from 2.01 to about 2.20 L/kWh cuts the electricity per pint by about 9%, which in the 4,000-pint example above is around $14 a year. Over a typical portable lifespan of several years that is a modest saving, so the designation is a clear win when the Most Efficient model costs about the same as the ordinary one and a poor reason on its own to pay a $100 premium. The AEOCKY in this guide costs more than a mainstream pump unit, so buy it for the pump and the longer coverage as much as for the efficiency.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Verify the Claim</h3>
          <p>
            Verify the claim before you rely on it. Listings on marketplaces sometimes say Most Efficient for a model that does not carry it, or quote an older year's recognition for a different revision. The only reliable check is the exact model number, including any suffix, in the ENERGY STAR product finder, where a Most Efficient field says yes or no.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="right-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Right-Sizing Saves More Than a Better IEF
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Removed Is the Biggest Variable</h3>
          <p>
            The biggest variable in what a dehumidifier costs to run is not its IEF but how much water it has to remove, which depends on how damp the space is and where you set the humidistat. A space that leaks moisture all summer will cost more to dry than a tight one, whatever machine you buy. Efficiency matters, but runtime matters more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Larger Classes Are More Efficient</h3>
          <p>
            Size affects efficiency directly. Because the 25-to-50-pint class must meet 2.01 L/kWh while small units need only 1.70, a typical certified 50-pint unit removes water with less electricity per pint than a typical certified 25-pint unit. Buying a smaller dehumidifier for a damp basement to save power usually backfires: it runs continuously, never reaches its setpoint and spends more electricity per pint along the way. Our <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement sizing guide</a> shows how to match capacity to dampness.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oversizing Costs Little</h3>
          <p>
            Oversizing costs little by comparison. A larger unit reaches the setpoint and cycles off, and the ENERGY STAR metric already counts the standby and fan-only power it uses between cycles. In a bedroom or small office, a small unit like the Levoit is the right tool; our <a href="/best-small-dehumidifier/">best small dehumidifier guide</a> covers more options in that class. In a basement, start at 35 pints and move to 50 if the space is genuinely damp.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut the Moisture at the Source</h3>
          <p>
            The cheapest kilowatt-hour is the one you never use. Extending downspouts, fixing grading, sealing rim joists, venting the dryer outdoors and running bathroom fans all reduce the moisture a dehumidifier has to remove. Our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> lists the fixes that cut the load at the source, and they pay back faster than any efficiency upgrade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-home" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Whole-Home and Ducted Units: The Efficiency Ceiling
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Highest IEFs on the List</h3>
          <p>
            The highest IEFs on the certified list belong to ducted whole-home units. In the November 2026 dataset, Santa Fe's largest whole-house models reach 3.12 and 3.26 L/kWh, the Ultra V100 in this guide is listed at 2.45, and Aprilaire's E-series units at 2.35. The best portable on the list reaches 2.24. Whole-home units are tested at 73°F and grouped by cabinet volume rather than capacity, so they are a separate category, but the comparison shows how much a larger coil and blower can gain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Payback Math</h3>
          <p>
            The economics are different, though. A whole-home unit costs roughly $1,700 to $2,700 before installation, and efficiency alone will not pay that back quickly. In a basement removing 6,000 pints a year, an E080 at 2.35 L/kWh would save around 200 kWh, or about $35 a year, compared with a 2.01 portable. Buy a whole-home unit for capacity, quiet operation, permanent drainage and the ability to dry a partitioned space or the whole house; treat the efficiency as a welcome bonus.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilating Models and the Ducted-vs-Portable Choice</h3>
          <p>
            Ventilating models such as the Ultra V100 add another factor. They bring in measured fresh air and dry it before it enters the house, which tight new homes often need, but drying humid outdoor air takes energy that a recirculating unit does not spend. In a leaky older house, a recirculating unit is usually the better fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are weighing a ducted system against portables, our <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/">whole-house vs portable dehumidifier</a> comparison covers installation, cost and coverage, and our <a href="/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/">best whole-house dehumidifier guide</a> reviews the leading models in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rebates" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Rebates, Tax Credits and Recycling Programs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Utility Rebates</h3>
          <p>
            Many electric utilities offer rebates on ENERGY STAR certified dehumidifiers, and some restrict them to Most Efficient models. The simplest way to find what is available is the ENERGY STAR Rebate Finder on energystar.gov, which searches by ZIP code, or the rebates page on your utility's website. Amounts vary widely by utility and change from year to year, so check before you buy rather than after.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Fine Print</h3>
          <p>
            Read the fine print. Most programs require the exact certified model number, a dated receipt and an application within a set window after purchase, and some only pay for units bought from participating retailers. A few utilities run instant discounts through their own online marketplaces instead of mail-in rebates. Where a rebate is limited to Most Efficient models, it can tip the balance toward a unit like the AEOCKY or Levoit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recycling Programs</h3>
          <p>
            Some utilities also run appliance recycling programs that pick up an old working dehumidifier, sometimes with a small payment. Old units built before the 2019 standards can use considerably more electricity than a certified replacement, and recycling through a program ensures the refrigerant is recovered rather than vented.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Federal Tax Credit</h3>
          <p>
            Do not count on a tax break. Portable dehumidifiers are not eligible for the federal residential energy efficiency tax credit, and a whole-home dehumidifier is not one of the equipment categories that credit covered. Any savings will come from utility rebates and from the lower electricity bill itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product-finder" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Check Any Dehumidifier in the Product Finder
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Search the Product Finder</h3>
          <p>
            The ENERGY STAR product finder on energystar.gov lists every currently certified dehumidifier. Search by brand or model number, and each record shows the water removal capacity under the current DOE test, the integrated energy factor, annual energy use, refrigerant type, whether the unit is connected-capable, the certification date and whether it carries the Most Efficient designation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Model Number Exactly</h3>
          <p>
            Match the model number exactly, including suffixes. Manufacturers often certify a family of revisions separately, and a listing may use wildcards for color or market codes. Retail names also differ from certified names: Santa Fe's Ultra V100, for instance, is certified under model number 4047100, and AEOCKY's LEO-003 appears as an additional model on a record filed under a much longer factory number. If you cannot find a model, it either is not certified under the current specification or is listed under a slightly different number, and it is worth asking the seller which.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Open Dataset</h3>
          <p>
            The same data is published as an open dataset on data.energystar.gov, which lets you download or sort the entire list. That is how we checked every figure in this guide, and it is the fastest way to answer questions like which 50-pint units beat the 2.01 floor or which models have built-in Wi-Fi.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut Through Listing Inflation</h3>
          <p>
            Use the finder to cut through listing inflation, too. Marketplace titles routinely quote pints at saturation, such as 140 or 150 pints a day, or coverage claims of 7,000 or 8,000 square feet, neither of which is part of the DOE test. The AEOCKY LEO-003, sold on a 140-pint headline, is a 44.94-pint dehumidifier in its ENERGY STAR record. The certified capacity is the number to compare.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="settings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Settings That Cut the Bill Further
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Humidistat Setting</h3>
          <p>
            The humidistat setting has more effect on your bill than any efficiency rating. Holding a basement at 40% instead of 50% can keep the compressor running for many more hours for little practical benefit. Fifty percent prevents mold and dust mites in most homes; our guide to <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> explains the seasonal adjustments.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Continuous Mode</h3>
          <p>
            Avoid continuous mode except after a leak or flood. Continuous operation ignores the humidistat and runs the compressor around the clock. Likewise, check whether your unit keeps its fan running after it reaches the setpoint; some models let you choose a mode where the fan cycles off with the compressor, which trims standby consumption.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance</h3>
          <p>
            Maintenance protects efficiency. A clogged filter or dusty coil restricts airflow, so the unit runs longer to remove the same water, and in a cool room it frosts sooner and spends more time defrosting. Rinse the filter every few weeks in heavy use and vacuum the coil fins once a season. Give the unit clearance on all sides and keep doors and windows to the space closed while it runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Switch It Off Off-Season</h3>
          <p>
            Finally, switch it off when the season is over. In many homes a heated basement stays below 50% through the winter without help, and a dehumidifier left running then wastes power. Check with a hygrometer before switching off. If you are still choosing a unit, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a> guide covers the wider field and how to pick a size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are ENERGY STAR dehumidifiers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for any unit that will run for months. A certified 50-pint model at 2.01 L/kWh uses about 20% less electricity per pint than one at the 1.60 federal minimum, which can save around $40 a year in a damp basement at 17 cents per kWh. Most big-brand units are certified at similar prices, so there is little reason to buy one that is not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good integrated energy factor for a dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on size. For portables up to 25 pints a day, ENERGY STAR requires 1.70 L/kWh and 1.85 or more is excellent. For 25 to 50 pints, the requirement is 2.01 and 2.20 or more earns Most Efficient. Whole-home units start at 2.22 and the best reach above 3.0 L/kWh.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does an ENERGY STAR dehumidifier cost to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 17 cents per kWh, a certified 50-pint unit costs about $40 to remove 1,000 pints of water. A damp basement that needs about 4,000 pints removed a year costs roughly $160. The ENERGY STAR annual figure for typical 50-pint units is 512 to 530 kWh, about $87 to $90, based on lighter use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most energy efficient dehumidifier for a basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among portables, look for a 25-to-50-pint unit with the Most Efficient designation and an IEF of at least 2.20, such as the AEOCKY LEO-003 at 2.21 with a built-in pump. For a permanent install, ducted units such as the Aprilaire E080 at 2.35 and Santa Fe Ultra V100 at 2.45 L/kWh are more efficient still, at a much higher price.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ENERGY STAR dehumidifiers qualify for rebates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, through your electric utility rather than the government. Many utilities offer rebates on certified dehumidifiers, and some only on Most Efficient models. Use the ENERGY STAR Rebate Finder or your utility's website, check the model list before buying, and keep the receipt. Portable dehumidifiers do not qualify for the federal energy efficiency tax credit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if a dehumidifier is ENERGY STAR certified?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Search the exact model number in the ENERGY STAR product finder on energystar.gov. Each certified model has a record with its capacity, integrated energy factor, annual kWh and Most Efficient status. Do not rely on the logo on old stock, because units built under the earlier Version 5.0 specification may not meet the current requirements.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is ENERGY STAR Most Efficient worth paying more for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only when the premium is small. Moving from 2.01 to 2.20 L/kWh cuts electricity per pint by about 9%, roughly $14 a year for a basement removing 4,000 pints. That is worth having when the Most Efficient model costs about the same as an ordinary one, but it would take years to recover a $100 premium.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying extra for a logo every rival has</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nearly every big-brand 35- and 50-pint dehumidifier is ENERGY STAR certified at exactly 2.01 L/kWh. Paying more for one because of the logo buys nothing its competitors lack. Compare the integrated energy factor in the product finder instead, and pay a premium only for a figure meaningfully above the 2.01 floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing old energy factors with current IEF</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spec sheets for older dehumidifiers quote an energy factor measured at 80°F that ignored standby and fan-only power. Those numbers look better than a current integrated energy factor measured at 65°F. Only compare efficiency figures from the current DOE test, which is what the ENERGY STAR product finder lists.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Most Efficient claim in a listing title</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Marketplace listings sometimes claim Most Efficient for a model that does not carry it, or quote an older revision. Look up the exact model number, including suffixes, in the ENERGY STAR product finder before paying extra. The Most Efficient field on the record is the only claim that counts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a smaller unit to save electricity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small dehumidifier in a damp basement runs nonstop, never reaches its setpoint and uses more electricity per pint, because the small class has a lower efficiency requirement than the 35- and 50-pint class. Size the unit to the dampness of the space; an adequately sized machine finishes the job and cycles off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the humidistat too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dropping the setpoint from 50% to 40% can keep the compressor running for many more hours for little benefit, wiping out the savings of a more efficient model. Fifty percent prevents mold and dust mites in most homes. Use the humidistat rather than continuous mode, and switch off once the season ends.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

